will represent partial assignments x[1] = v1 , . . . , x[m] = vm as sequences\n[v1 , . . . , vm ] for easier manipulation. We will also often use the same symbol to denote\nthe sequence and its corresponding set.\n\n\n6.3.1      Definite Moves\nUnder certain circumstances, one particular choice for the next customer will become\nso good that it is at least as good as any other choice. In this case, we can pick that\nchoice and prune all other choices. We call this a definite move.\n\nTheorem 6.3.6. Suppose S is some sequence, and q ∈\n                                                 / S is a customer such that:\nS ++ [q] is k-playable, and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S). If S has an extension that uses\n≤ k stacks, then S ++ [q] also has an extension that uses ≤ k stacks.\n\nProof. Suppose U ≡ S ++ [c1 , c2 , . . . , cm , q, cm+1 , . . . , cn ] uses ≤ k stacks. We claim\nthat U 0 ≡ S ++ [q, c1 , . . . , cm , cm+1 , . . . , cn ] also uses ≤ k stacks. The two sequences\ndiffer only in the placement of q. The number of stacks which are open at any time\nbefore, or any time after the set of customers {c1 , c2 , . . . , cm , q} are played is identical\nfor U and U 0 , since it only depending on the set of customers closed and not the\norder. The number of open stacks when q is played in U 0 is also ≤ k, since S ++ [q] is\nk-playable. Finally, the number of open stacks when S +\n                                                      + [q, c1 , c2 , . . . , ci ] is played in\nU 0 is always less than or equal to the number of open stacks when S ++ [c1 , c2 , . . . , ci ]\nis played in U , because we have at most open(q, S) extra stacks open, but at least\nclose(q, S) extra stacks closed. So U 0 uses ≤ k stacks at all times. \u0003\n\nCorollary 6.3.7. Suppose in the branch and bound algorithm, we are currently seek-\ning solutions that use ≤ k stacks. If at some search node, S is the current sequence,\nand there exists q ∈\n                   / S such that: S ++ [q] is k-playable and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S),\nthen it is correct to prune all choices other than q.\n\f6.3. IMPROVING THE SEARCH                                                                  143\n\n\n\n6.3.2     Better Moves\nSimilarly, there are situations where one choice is at least as good as another, and we\ncan prune the one that is not as good. We call this a better move.\n\nTheorem 6.3.8. Suppose S is some sequence, and q, r ∈\n                                                    / S are customers such that:\nS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are both k-playable, and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}). If\n + [r] has an extension that uses ≤ k stacks, then S +\nS+                                                   + [q] also has an extension that\nuses ≤ k stacks.\n\nProof. Suppose U ≡ S ++ [r, c1 , c2 , .., cm , q, cm+1 , . . . , cn ] uses ≤ k stacks. We have\nclose(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}), so after r is played, q becomes a\ndefinite move. By the same argument as the previous proof, U 0 ≡ S +\n                                                                   + [r, q, c1 , . . . , cn ]\nuses ≤ k stacks. Now, consider U 00 ≡ S +\n                                        + [q, r, c1 , . . . , cn ], where we swap the positions\nof q and r. Consider the stacks which are open when r is played in U 00 compared to\nwhen r is played in U 0 . The number of new open stacks is exactly open(q, S ∪ {r}),\nand the number of new closed stacks is exactly close(q, S). So playing r in U 00 uses no\nmore stacks than playing r in U 0 . Also, the number of open stacks when q is played\nin U 00 is ≤ k since S ++ [q] is k-playable. Thus U 00 uses ≤ k stacks at all times. \u0003\n\nCorollary 6.3.9. Suppose in the branch and bound algorithm, we are currently seek-\ning solutions that use ≤ k stacks. If at some search node, S is the current sequence,\nand there exists q, r ∈\n                      / S such that: S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are both k-playable, and\nclose(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r