;\n624-`IsRepairedBetter.isBetter` shows it implies the code's premise). By Theorem\n625-4.8 this is one matching at the child.\n626-\n627-**Theorem 4.14 (the repaired better move is sound).** If $r, q \\notin S$,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ae08bb86-d553-438a-9cbf-85e42a7944af","uuid":"6727bdd2-4a04-40bf-b6e5-2ec67c3e3291","timestamp":"2026-10-03T00:30:41.066Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"599:**The thesis's form.** Chu (2011) states the better move as \"**Theorem 6.3.8.**\n600-Suppose S is some sequence, and q, r ∉ S are customers such that: S ++ [q] and\n601-S ++ [r, q] are both k-playable, and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}). If\n602-S ++ [r] has an extension that uses ≤ k stacks, then S ++ [q] also has an\n603-extension that uses ≤ k stacks\" (p. 143). The premise takes close at $S$, not at\n604-$S \\cup \\{r\\}$. The proof derives the CP premise from it\n605-(\"close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}), so after r is played, q\n606-becomes a definite move\") and then appeals to Theorem 6.3.6, so it fails at the\n607-same step. Counterexample 4.13 does not cover it, but the graph of\n608-Counterexample 4.5 does: take $S = \\{2\\}$, $r = 3$, $q = 0$, $k = 6$. Then\n609-$\\mathrm{close}(0, S) = 3 \\ge 2 = \\mathrm{open}(0, S \\cup \\{3\\})$, both\n610-$S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [0]$ and $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [3, 0]$ are playable (costs 6\n611-and 5), $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [3]$ extends by\n612-$1, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11$, and $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [0]$ has no\n613-extension, which is Counterexample 4.5's child. Under the literal reading of\n614-close the premise is weaker and the same witness serves; a second witness,\n615-$S = \\{1\\}$, $r = 6$, $q = 12$, holds under the literal reading only.\n616-(Lean: `chuThesis_theorem638_false`, `chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal`,\n617-`chuThesis_theorem638_literal_witness`, `Search/ChuThesis.lean`; the brute\n618-force over all $2^{14}$ sets that found the witnesses is\