{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfePmz7FYZTJ7MRXpwGao","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 02 is done: Chu's 2011 thesis Theorem 6.3.8 is now proved false in Lean, in the new file `lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/ChuThesis.lean`, with no `sorry`. The gate passes: Lean builds, the only `sorry` is still the §24 conjecture, MOSP pytest passes 1,364 tests and pathwidth_solver pytest passes 114. The split run's logs have no `!!!` line.\n\n**One of our published claims was wrong, and I fixed it.** Item 01 said Fink (2012) restates Chu & Stuckey's Theorem 1 with the same premise. It doesn't. Fink counts only the dominated customers with a smaller index than q, so q itself is never counted. That makes the premise strictly stronger, and our 14-customer counterexample graph does not refute it under any labelling. I corrected this in `paper2/prior_art_counterexample.md` and noted it at the top of PROGRESS.md.\n\n**Theorem 6.3.8 (the better move).** Its premise is `close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r})`, which differs from the CP paper's. It is false on the same graph at k = 6, under both readings of `close`.\n- **Witness:** S = {2}, r = 3, q = 0, with close 3 ≥ open 2. The thesis's proof goes through Theorem 6.3.6, so it breaks at the same state as the CP counterexample.\n- **Second witness for the literal reading only:** S = {1}, r = 6, q = 12.\n- **Wording:** quoted from the thesis's PDF, §6.3, pp. 141–143.\n\n**Theorem 6.3.6** is CP Theorem 1 word for word. It is recorded under the thesis's number as `chuThesis_theorem636_false` (and the literal-reading version), which refer to the existing CP refutations.\n\n**Fink's version is also false, which goes beyond the item.** A random search over about 41,700 graphs on 6–12 vertices found no failure. Adding one more customer to the counterexample graph, a third twin of customers 3 and 4, gives a 15-customer graph that refutes it at S = {2, 3}, q = 14, k = 6. That is `fink_theorem1_false` in Lean, with an independent brute-force check in `paper2/fink_check.py` (run `python3 -m paper2.fink_check`) and three tests.\n\n**Text updated:**\n- `paper2/prior_art_counterexample.md` has a new section with a table of the Lean theorems.\n- `paper2/revised_algorithm.md` now cites the thesis beside the CP paper wherever a rule is stated or refuted. It has a new paragraph refuting Theorem 6.3.8 and one on Fink.\n- `paper2/axiom_check.lean` lists all six new theorems; their axioms are `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` only.\n\nThe item is marked `[x]`, PROGRESS.md is updated to 2/9, and nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2113,"cache_read_input_tokens":140991,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2113,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ae08bb86-d553-438a-9cbf-85e42a7944af","uuid":"a09034a3-7204-4aa5-8ae1-794ed674b735","timestamp":"2026-10-03T00:56:14.917Z","request_id":"req_011CfePmyihGp8JryUncFN5D"}
